.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NSHDECK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nshdeck
.Nd web configurator for nshd
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dv
.Op Fl s Ar socket
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
answers the requests of a web page, which
.Xr httpd 8
passes it over FastCGI, with what
.Xr nshd 8
answers.
The page has seven workspaces: an overview, with the box's ports drawn
as its face shows them, each with a link light and an activity light,
a readout of how many ports are up, the interfaces and addresses, and
the last change, and the logical interfaces grouped by what they are on;
network, routing, security and services, each with its shows,
as tables; in network an interface's settings, which can be changed
and staged into the candidate as lines of configuration; in routing,
security and services the daemons there, a row for each routing table
each is in, with whether the configuration enables it and whether it
runs, and the one chosen with its rules as text, which can be changed
and staged into the candidate as its edit in
.Xr nsh 8
does, and its live operations; the system
and its changes, in four topics: changes, where configure is entered
and lines of configuration are typed, as
.Xr nsh 8
takes them, beside a view of the changes, the candidate, the running
or the saved configuration; history, the saved configurations as rows,
any of which can be shown or loaded into the candidate, nshd's notices,
and the logs followed; jobs and MOTD, root's scheduled jobs and the
message of the day, as the daemons are; and live operations, each
confirmed before it runs; and a terminal, where
.Xr nsh 8
runs as the user, which xterm.js draws.
Tab completes a line as in
.Xr nsh 8 ,
and ? lists what may come next.
The page follows
.Nm nshd Ns 's
notices and logs as they come, counts down a commit waiting to be
confirmed, and draws the interfaces again when they change.
Its strip has Compare, Validate and Commit: the changes,
.Cm commit check ,
and
.Cm commit .
While the overview is shown, it asks for the interfaces every 5 seconds:
a port's activity light is on if the port counted packets since the
last time.
Each user logs in with a name and password, as
.Nm nshd
checks them, and has the permissions of the user's login class (see
.Cm login class
in
.Xr nsh 8 ) .
root may not log in.
.Pp
.Nm
is privilege separated:
.Bl -tag -width "session"
.It parent
Starts as root, binds the socket, and starts the other two.
Then it runs as _nshweb, a member of the group nshadmin, and does one
thing: when session asks, it connects to
.Nm nshd ,
and passes the connection on.
.Nm nshd
takes a client of _nshweb's uid as the web configurator's, which has
no permissions until it logs in.
.It front
Runs as _nshdeck in a
.Xr chroot 2 .
It reads the requests
.Xr httpd 8
sends, and checks each one whole before session sees it.
.It session
Runs as _nshweb in a
.Xr chroot 2 .
It has a connection to
.Nm nshd
for each user logged in, known by the token in the user's cookie, and
writes each answer as JSON.
It completes lines itself, with
.Xr nsh 8 Ns 's
code: it asks
.Nm nshd
for the command tables once, and for the names of interfaces, groups,
bridges and routing tables on each completion.
It asks
.Nm nshd
for each terminal a user opens;
.Nm nshd
starts
.Xr nsh 8
as the user and passes back its pseudo-terminal.
.El
.Pp
If one of them ends,
.Nm
ends.
.Pp
The last part of a request's path names it.
A GET has its fields in its query, and a POST in a form body, of type
application/x-www-form-urlencoded; a field may be given once.
Every request except a stream,
.Cm events
or
.Cm terminal ,
must have the header
.Ql X-Nshdeck: 1 ,
which a page of another site cannot send without asking the browser
first.
Every request except
.Cm login
needs the cookie that
.Cm login
sets.
.Bl -tag -width "configure"
.It Cm login
POST, with
.Ar user
and
.Ar password .
Logs the user in, and sets the cookie nshdeck, HttpOnly, Secure and
SameSite=Strict, to a token of 32 random bytes.
Answers the user's name and permissions.
.It Cm logout
POST.
Logs the user out.
.Nm nshd
forgets the login, and the candidate of
.Cm configure private
with it.
.It Cm whoami
GET.
The user's name and permissions.
.It Cm show
GET, with
.Ar words ,
as
.Xr nsh 8
shows them, such as
.Ql interface
or
.Ql route inet 0 .
They are asked of
.Nm nshd Ns 's
system and, if it does not answer them, of its shows, as
.Xr nsh 8
asks them.
The answer has the records
.Nm nshd
sent, each an object with its kind and fields.
.It Cm config
GET, with
.Ar what :
running, startup, candidate, diff, or rollback, with
.Ar n
or not; or changes, with
.Ar n .
The configuration, as text: rollback without
.Ar n
lists those saved, and changes is what configuration
.Ar n
changed from the one saved before it, as
.Cm show rollback Ar n Cm diff
in
.Xr nsh 8
shows it.
.It Cm configure
POST, with
.Ar mode :
private, shared, exclusive or terminal.
.It Cm line
POST, with
.Ar line :
a line of configuration, in configure.
.It Cm exit
POST.
Leaves configure, or the context a line entered.
.It Cm commit
POST, with
.Ar check
1 to check the candidate only,
.Ar minutes
for
.Cm commit confirmed ,
and a
.Ar comment .
.It Cm rollback
POST, with
.Ar n .
.It Cm op
POST, with
.Ar line ,
an operation such as
.Ql flush routes ,
and its
.Ar rtable .
.It Cm rules
GET, with
.Ar daemon ,
named as
.Xr nsh 8
names it, such as pf or bgp, and its
.Ar rtable .
The daemon's rules in the candidate of
.Cm configure private ,
in
.Ar output .
.It Cm setrules
POST, with
.Ar daemon ,
.Ar rtable
and
.Ar text ,
up to 1 MB.
Sets the daemon's rules in the candidate, as the daemon's
.Cm edit
in
.Xr nsh 8
does; with no
.Ar text ,
the rules go.
Its body may be 3 MB, and 4 such bodies may arrive at once.
.It Cm complete
GET, with
.Ar line ,
and
.Ar help
1 or 0.
Completes the line at its end, in the user's mode, as Tab does in
.Xr nsh 8 .
Each record is of kind insert, with the text to add to the line, or of
kind choice, with a word and its help, if it has one.
With
.Ar help
1, nothing is inserted and every choice is listed.
.It Cm events
GET, with
.Ar logs ,
names joined by commas: daemon, messages, authlog or pflog.
A stream of events, of type text/event-stream:
.Nm nshd Ns 's
notices, of commits, commits confirmed, commits gone back,
.Cm configure exclusive
taken and left, and interfaces and routes changed; and the lines of
each log named, from its last lines.
authlog needs the admin permission.
A comment is written to the stream every 30 seconds, so that
.Xr httpd 8
does not close it.
If the browser falls more than 1 MB behind, the oldest events it has
not been sent are dropped, and it is sent a dropped event in their
place, so that it asks again for what it shows.
.It Cm terminal
GET, with
.Ar rows
and
.Ar cols ,
each from 1 to 1000, 24 and 80 if not given.
A terminal:
.Xr nsh 8
on a pseudo-terminal, as the user, as
.Nm nshd
starts it.
A stream of events, as
.Cm events
is: first a terminal event, with
.Ar n ,
the terminal's number, from 1 to 4; then an out event for what
.Xr nsh 8
writes, as it comes, in base64; and at the end an end event, with
.Ar text ,
which says why it ended.
The stream is sent only as fast as the browser reads it, and nothing
of it is dropped.
The terminal ends when its stream does,
.Xr nsh 8
ends, or the user logs out.
A user may have 4 terminals.
.It Cm type
POST, with
.Ar terminal ,
its number,
.Ar keys ,
what was typed in it, up to 1023 bytes, and
.Ar rows
and
.Ar cols ,
for its new size.
What was typed goes to
.Xr nsh 8
as it takes it; past 64 KB that it has not taken, the request is
refused, with 503.
.El
.Pp
Each answer except an event stream's is a JSON object.
One that
.Nm nshd
answered has
.Ar records ,
.Ar output ,
what
.Nm nshd
printed,
.Ar context ,
where the user is in configure,
.Ar status
and
.Ar ok ;
it is sent with HTTP status 200, even when
.Nm nshd
refused the request.
Other answers have an
.Ar error
and an HTTP status that says why: 400 for a field that cannot be taken,
401 when the user is not logged in or the login failed, 403 for a POST
without the header, 404 for an unknown request, 405 for the wrong
method, 413 for a body over 8192 bytes, or 3 MB for
.Cm setrules ,
415 for a POST that is not a form, and 503 when
.Nm nshd
does not answer, or too many users are logged in, a user has too many
requests waiting or terminals, or too many rules are arriving.
.Pp
A user's requests go to
.Nm nshd
one at a time, and at most 16 wait.
A long answer is read from
.Nm nshd
only as fast as the client reads it.
A user with no event stream or terminal open and no request for 30
minutes is logged out.
At most 64 users may be logged in, and
.Nm
takes at most 128 connections.
.Pp
.Nm
needs the users _nshweb and _nshdeck, which may not be root or share a
uid, and the group www, which
.Xr httpd 8
runs as.
The socket is root's, mode 0660, group www.
In
.Xr httpd.conf 5 ,
with the page at
.Pa https://router/nshdeck/ :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
server "router" {
	listen on * tls port 443
	connection max request body 3146752
	location "/nshdeck/api/*" {
		fastcgi socket "/run/nshdeck.sock"
	}
	location "/nshdeck/*" {
		root "/htdocs"
	}
}
.Ed
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize, and log to standard error.
.It Fl s Ar socket
The socket, instead of
.Pa /var/www/run/nshdeck.sock .
.It Fl v
Produce more verbose output.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/www/run/nshdeck.sockXX" -compact
.It Pa /var/www/run/nshdeck.sock
the socket
.Xr httpd 8
connects to
.It Pa /var/www/htdocs/nshdeck
the page
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr httpd.conf 5 ,
.Xr httpd 8 ,
.Xr nsh 8 ,
.Xr nshd 8
